1753
01:00:52,753 --> 01:00:53,982
Are you going to faint?

1754
01:00:53,983 --> 01:00:55,623
- Don't mind me even if I faint.
- Here.

1755
01:00:55,853 --> 01:00:58,893
Please do not mind me even if I black out.

1756
01:00:59,123 --> 01:01:01,299
- I'll take control of myself.
- You'll get dehydrated.

1757
01:01:01,323 --> 01:01:02,622
I'm worried you'll faint from dehydration...

1758
01:01:02,623 --> 01:01:03,662
when we have to play a game.

1759
01:01:03,663 --> 01:01:04,833
I'll handle it myself.

1760
01:01:05,633 --> 01:01:08,133
- I'm worried if you'll be okay.
- It's hot indeed.

1761
01:01:08,533 --> 01:01:11,102
You know what I'm wearing this.

1762
01:01:11,103 --> 01:01:12,902
The "Tazza" poster is red.

1763
01:01:12,903 --> 01:01:13,932
(The color of the "Tazza" poster is red.)

1764
01:01:13,933 --> 01:01:15,043
I can't take it off.

1765
01:01:15,273 --> 01:01:17,842
You could've worn something thin in red.

1766
01:01:17,843 --> 01:01:19,743
Gosh. The fabric is so thick.

1767
01:01:20,313 --> 01:01:22,613
Really. It's suede.

1768
01:01:23,383 --> 01:01:25,382
(His passionate self won't give up the suede under the blazing sun.)

1769
01:01:25,383 --> 01:01:26,682
It's okay.

1770
01:01:26,683 --> 01:01:28,523
- I'll be determined until the end.
- Okay.

1771
01:01:29,023 --> 01:01:31,253
- That's it.
- It's here. We have to catch it.

1772
01:01:31,853 --> 01:01:32,892
Hello, sir.

1773
01:01:32,893 --> 01:01:34,122
- Good afternoon.
- Thanks.

1774
01:01:34,123 --> 01:01:35,393
- Good afternoon.
- Yes.

1775
01:01:36,263 --> 01:01:37,962
- Gosh. It feels so much better.
- Right?

1776
01:01:37,963 --> 01:01:40,163
- Can I turn the AC on for me?
- Yes. Just one, though.

1777
01:01:40,363 --> 01:01:41,462
- You can't keep two.
- Do you want to sit here?

1778
01:01:41,463 --> 01:01:43,162
- Just a little bit.
- Sit here.

1779
01:01:43,163 --> 01:01:44,433
It feels much better.

1780
01:01:45,303 --> 01:01:46,473
You look cool, though.

1781
01:01:46,703 --> 01:01:49,672
Fashionistas don't take off their clothes.

1782
01:01:49,673 --> 01:01:50,873
- Is that so?
- You know it.

1783
01:01:51,043 --> 01:01:52,523
- I'll keep it until the end.
- Okay.

1784
01:01:53,913 --> 01:01:55,643
What's your MBTI, Jae Won?

1785
01:01:55,843 --> 01:01:57,853
- I'm an INFP.
- I see. An INFP.

1786
01:01:57,913 --> 01:01:59,982
You're an INFP. Yes.

1787
01:01:59,983 --> 01:02:01,823
- What about you?
- I'm an ISFP.

1788
01:02:02,683 --> 01:02:04,493
I'm the lazy type.

1789
01:02:05,423 --> 01:02:07,823
- Are you not a planner, then?
- Not at all.

1790
01:02:08,523 --> 01:02:10,192
- Do you plan things?
- I don't.

1791
01:02:10,193 --> 01:02:11,633
- You're not a planner either.
- No.

1792
01:02:11,833 --> 01:02:14,633
- What about you?
- I make plans subtly.

1793
01:02:15,103 --> 01:02:16,833
I've never planned anything in life.

1794
01:02:18,673 --> 01:02:20,372
Is it "Carpe diem," then?

1795
01:02:20,373 --> 01:02:21,702
Do you appreciate every moment?

1796
01:02:21,703 --> 01:02:22,703
I don't appreciate it.

1797
01:02:23,103 --> 01:02:24,342
- What do you do, then?
- I just...

1798
01:02:24,343 --> 01:02:25,543
What do you do, then?

1799
01:02:26,473 --> 01:02:29,043
Don't you know what I do? I work like this.

1800
01:02:29,183 --> 01:02:30,382
I just do what I'm supposed to do.

1801
01:02:30,383 --> 01:02:32,312
Yu Jae Seok is shooting something.

1802
01:02:32,313 --> 01:02:34,013
He's on the bus.

1803
01:02:34,053 --> 01:02:35,282
- What?
- They're on the bus together.

1804
01:02:35,283 --> 01:02:36,552
What is it?

1805
01:02:36,553 --> 01:02:38,492
- I'm a fan, Jae Seok.
- Who?

1806
01:02:38,493 --> 01:02:40,553
Someone reported this to the radio.

1807
01:02:41,363 --> 01:02:42,722
They're talking about us.

1808
01:02:42,723 --> 01:02:44,533
- This is crazy.
- How are we on the radio?

1809
01:02:44,833 --> 01:02:46,262
Isn't this the first time ever?

1810
01:02:46,263 --> 01:02:47,293
What's happening?

1811
01:02:48,663 --> 01:02:50,902
- A passenger must've texted it.
- Did they?

1812
01:02:50,903 --> 01:02:51,903
(A passenger on the same bus told the radio station right away.)

1813
01:02:51,904 --> 01:02:53,273
Incheon people are so kind.

1814
01:02:53,803 --> 01:02:56,102
- Incheon people are full of love.
- I love them.

1815
01:02:56,103 --> 01:02:57,302
They send the story out right after encountering us.

1816
01:02:57,303 --> 01:02:58,813
In Incheon,

1817
01:02:59,513 --> 01:03:00,972
they embrace us.

1818
01:03:00,973 --> 01:03:03,142
It's charming, isn't it? When we get off,

1819
01:03:03,143 --> 01:03:04,612
it'll be very artistic.

1820
01:03:04,613 --> 01:03:06,389
- It's the Culture and Arts Center.
- The Culture and Arts Center.

1821
01:03:06,413 --> 01:03:08,082
- We need to go further.
- I think it's the next stop.

1822
01:03:08,083 --> 01:03:09,283
- You're right.
- Right?

1823
01:03:09,823 --> 01:03:12,152
- It's from Jang Woo Shik.
- Woo Shik.

1824
01:03:12,153 --> 01:03:15,592
"Every day, I stand at the very back of the 100 people,"

1825
01:03:15,593 --> 01:03:17,992
"and I lead the 1-hour-and-30-minute march..."

1826
01:03:17,993 --> 01:03:20,433
"with a thump after a thump."

1827
01:03:22,403 --> 01:03:25,832
"With a gesture light as a feather, I roar like thunder..."

1828
01:03:25,833 --> 01:03:28,602
"while securing the same spot for 20 years."

1829
01:03:28,603 --> 01:03:32,243
"Dear Whenever Possible, please be my first movement today."

1830
01:03:32,613 --> 01:03:35,013
- I see. He's a musician.
- He plays an instrument or music.

1831
01:03:35,343 --> 01:03:36,582
At the back,

1832
01:03:36,583 --> 01:03:38,152
(Is the second Whenever Guest a musician who plays an instrument?)

1833
01:03:38,153 --> 01:03:40,313
- he might play the bass drum.
- The bass drum. Right.

1834
01:03:41,823 --> 01:03:44,692
- That was entertaining to watch.
- It's very dynamic.

1835
01:03:44,693 --> 01:03:47,493
- Incheon is very dynamic.
- It's great.

1836
01:03:48,023 --> 01:03:49,463
Living here is a rollercoaster ride.

1837
01:03:50,423 --> 01:03:52,162
It's different from day to day.

1838
01:03:52,163 --> 01:03:53,203
(Incheon is never boring.)

1839
01:03:53,433 --> 01:03:54,433
Hold on.

1840
01:03:55,303 --> 01:03:57,132
Do you even know where you're going?

1841
01:03:57,133 --> 01:03:58,202
Where is it? Tell us.

1842
01:03:58,203 --> 01:03:59,203
Hold on.

1843
01:04:00,003 --> 01:04:02,103
- I like it.
- This place is lovely.

1844
01:04:02,703 --> 01:04:03,873
It's nice and cool.

1845
01:04:04,043 --> 01:04:05,043
Try it.

1846
01:04:06,113 --> 01:04:07,113
Should I try this one?

1847
01:04:07,114 --> 01:04:09,043
(He smoothly joins him.)

1848
01:04:12,283 --> 01:04:13,783
My lower back. Gosh.

1849
01:04:14,113 --> 01:04:15,252
(Standing up)

1850
01:04:15,253 --> 01:04:17,793
I can't do it. I'll get sweaty.

1851
01:04:18,323 --> 01:04:19,323
He'll get sweaty.

1852
01:04:19,993 --> 01:04:21,993
- We have to go to the west gate.
- The west gate?

1853
01:04:22,193 --> 01:04:23,392
- Is it this way?
- Yes.

1854
01:04:23,393 --> 01:04:25,833
Gosh. Since it's a large region, I can't tell where I'm at.

1855
01:04:27,393 --> 01:04:28,703
(Glancing)

1856
01:04:28,903 --> 01:04:30,403
Jae Won is always so quiet...

1857
01:04:30,733 --> 01:04:32,073
and walks two steps behind.

1858
01:04:33,433 --> 01:04:35,793
It's hard to squeeze myself between you to be in the frame.

1859
01:04:36,003 --> 01:04:37,172
- It feels...
- Come on.

1860
01:04:37,173 --> 01:04:39,272
- he's two steps behind us.
- Stop going behind us.

1861
01:04:39,273 --> 01:04:41,582
- Why are you always behind us?
- Walk in front of us.

1862
01:04:41,583 --> 01:04:42,713
(Bravely leading the group)

1863
01:04:42,983 --> 01:04:44,582
- Let's go this way, Jae Won.
- Okay.

1864
01:04:44,583 --> 01:04:45,583
Hey, it's the west gate.

1865
01:04:46,213 --> 01:04:47,452
This is the west gate.

1866
01:04:47,453 --> 01:04:48,582
(They've arrived at the second spare time spot, )

1867
01:04:48,583 --> 01:04:49,943
(Incheon Culture and Arts Center.)

1868
01:04:50,153 --> 01:04:51,153
Gosh. It's hot.

1869
01:04:51,154 --> 01:04:52,522
(Enter the west gate and come all the way down the left hallway.)

1870
01:04:52,523 --> 01:04:53,552
Gosh. It's hot.

1871
01:04:53,553 --> 01:04:55,162
Good afternoon. Hi.

1872
01:04:55,163 --> 01:04:57,932
- We have to turn left...
- It's all the way down, right?

1873
01:04:57,933 --> 01:04:59,563
And go all the way down.

1874
01:05:02,133 --> 01:05:03,702
- What? Good afternoon.
- Hello.

1875
01:05:03,703 --> 01:05:05,072
- Woo Shik?
- Yes.

1876
01:05:05,073 --> 01:05:06,802
- Nice to meet you.
- Gosh. Good afternoon.

1877
01:05:06,803 --> 01:05:08,172
- Good afternoon.
- Yes.

1878
01:05:08,173 --> 01:05:09,702
- Nice to meet you. Gosh.
- Nice to meet you too.

1879
01:05:09,703 --> 01:05:10,703
(Whenever Guest is waiting for them at the end of the hallway.)

1880
01:05:10,704 --> 01:05:12,043
- Hello.
- Nice to meet you.

1881
01:05:12,273 --> 01:05:14,112
Is this the Incheon Philharmonic Orchestra?

1882
01:05:14,113 --> 01:05:15,612
Yes, it's the Incheon Metropolitan City Arts Troupe.

1883
01:05:15,613 --> 01:05:17,613
The Incheon Metropolitan City Arts Troupe.

1884
01:05:18,353 --> 01:05:20,152
My favorite actors are here.

1885
01:05:20,153 --> 01:05:21,252
- Gosh.
- Really.

1886
01:05:21,253 --> 01:05:23,452
- Let's go inside and talk.
- Yes. Follow me.

1887
01:05:23,453 --> 01:05:25,153
- It's a bit confusing in here.
- I see.

1888
01:05:26,253 --> 01:05:27,822
The practice room for the orchestra is on the left.

1889
01:05:27,823 --> 01:05:28,962
- Is it here?
- Yes.

1890
01:05:28,963 --> 01:05:30,092
(He's a member of the Incheon Philharmonic Orchestra.)

1891
01:05:30,093 --> 01:05:31,433
Please come this way.

1892
01:05:32,293 --> 01:05:33,492
- Hello.
- Gosh. It's nice and cold.

1893
01:05:33,493 --> 01:05:34,532
It's cold, right?

1894
01:05:34,533 --> 01:05:35,732
(The practice room is big and pleasant.)

1895
01:05:35,733 --> 01:05:36,802
Gosh. I feel better now.

1896
01:05:36,803 --> 01:05:38,232
Gosh. It's so cool.

1897
01:05:38,233 --> 01:05:40,933
The percussionists sit here.

1898
01:05:41,573 --> 01:05:44,543
I mainly play the timpani.

1899
01:05:44,673 --> 01:05:45,743
- Goodness.
- The timpani.

1900
01:05:46,513 --> 01:05:47,942
(Usually, the practice room for the Incheon Philharmonic...)

1901
01:05:47,943 --> 01:05:49,143
(is filled with music.)

1902
01:05:49,713 --> 01:05:50,982
(Including violins, )

1903
01:05:50,983 --> 01:05:52,612
(cellos, )

1904
01:05:52,613 --> 01:05:53,653
(and horns, )

1905
01:05:53,953 --> 01:05:56,683
(various instruments create a harmony.)

1906
01:05:57,183 --> 01:05:59,593
(What makes the rumbling sound at the very back...)

1907
01:06:00,493 --> 01:06:03,493
(is Whenever Guest Woo Shik's timpani.)

1908
01:06:05,163 --> 01:06:06,163
(Rumbling)

1909
01:06:06,433 --> 01:06:11,403
(A deep, resonant sound that brings all sounds together)

1910
01:06:11,573 --> 01:06:13,903
(Timpani, the heart of an orchestra)

1911
01:06:15,403 --> 01:06:16,802
You are indeed at the very back.

1912
01:06:16,803 --> 01:06:18,942
Yes. Most of the time,

1913
01:06:18,943 --> 01:06:21,713
I stand face-to-face with the conductor.

1914
01:06:22,213 --> 01:06:24,143
- Will you play for us?
- Yes.

1915
01:06:26,183 --> 01:06:28,183
(Rumbling)

1916
01:06:34,123 --> 01:06:35,923
(Surprised)

1917
01:06:38,733 --> 01:06:41,232
(The magnificent sound fills up the huge practice room.)

1918
01:06:41,233 --> 01:06:44,103
(Amazed)

1919
01:06:48,543 --> 01:06:50,843
(Controlling its pitch with a pedal)

1920
01:06:55,713 --> 01:06:57,542
(They can't help but clap.)

1921
01:06:57,543 --> 01:06:58,583
Awesome.

1922
01:06:59,253 --> 01:07:00,552
That was awesome.

1923
01:07:00,553 --> 01:07:02,152
- It rolled.
- Yes.

1924
01:07:02,153 --> 01:07:04,052
(Timpani's magnificent charms)

1925
01:07:04,053 --> 01:07:05,122
Gosh. It was so cool.

1926
01:07:05,123 --> 01:07:06,203
- Yes.
- Let's go that way.

1927
01:07:06,653 --> 01:07:08,362
- At the percussion section, - Look at the bass drum.

1928
01:07:08,363 --> 01:07:10,092
- we have drums.
- It's a percussion section.

1929
01:07:10,093 --> 01:07:11,893
- There's a cymbal too.
- There are pedals.

1930
01:07:12,733 --> 01:07:13,992
This is a marimba.

1931
01:07:13,993 --> 01:07:16,302
Yes. This instrument is called a marimba.

1932
01:07:16,303 --> 01:07:17,543
(Jae Seok notices the marimba.)

1933
01:07:17,733 --> 01:07:20,332
I don't usually play it.

1934
01:07:20,333 --> 01:07:21,373
You don't, but...

1935
01:07:24,473 --> 01:07:25,612
(Amazed)

1936
01:07:25,613 --> 01:07:26,872
It sounds so pretty.

1937
01:07:26,873 --> 01:07:28,073
It has such a beautiful sound.

1938
01:07:31,553 --> 01:07:35,253
(Its beautiful sound makes them focus.)

1939
01:07:36,823 --> 01:07:37,823
(Darn it.)

1940
01:07:37,953 --> 01:07:40,092
(He made a mistake because it has been a while.)

1941
01:07:40,093 --> 01:07:41,093
"Darn it."

1942
01:07:41,863 --> 01:07:44,662
- Anyway, let's sit and talk.
- Yes.

1943
01:07:44,663 --> 01:07:46,292
(Looking around)

1944
01:07:46,293 --> 01:07:48,462
I didn't expect Yo Han to come.

1945
01:07:48,463 --> 01:07:51,372
Nice to meet you. I performed in a play here.

1946
01:07:51,373 --> 01:07:52,402
- Really?
- Here?

1947
01:07:52,403 --> 01:07:54,032
- When I was in middle school.
- Here?

1948
01:07:54,033 --> 01:07:56,602
- I see.
- Son of Incheon came here.

1949
01:07:56,603 --> 01:08:01,013
Anyway, we're here to visit the orchestra.

1950
01:08:01,213 --> 01:08:05,212
Is the practice usually over by this time?

1951
01:08:05,213 --> 01:08:07,952
Today, we were done a bit early.

1952
01:08:07,953 --> 01:08:10,882
Usually, we practice from 10am to 3pm.

1953
01:08:10,883 --> 01:08:12,322
- Those are the practice hours.
- Yes.

1954
01:08:12,323 --> 01:08:15,392
There are so many different instruments.

1955
01:08:15,393 --> 01:08:18,732
Why did you choose percussion?

1956
01:08:18,733 --> 01:08:20,992
I had to go to college when I was a high school student.

1957
01:08:20,993 --> 01:08:24,002
I studied hard in my sophomore year, but I wasn't good at it.

1958
01:08:24,003 --> 01:08:27,472
Fortunately, my older sister was majoring in percussion.

1959
01:08:27,473 --> 01:08:29,302
- She played the instrument.
- Yes.

1960
01:08:29,303 --> 01:08:30,472
For other instruments,

1961
01:08:30,473 --> 01:08:31,642
- you have to have your own.
- Right.

1962
01:08:31,643 --> 01:08:33,763
But as for percussion, you can share the instruments.

1963
01:08:34,243 --> 01:08:36,313
That's why I majored it with her.

1964
01:08:37,113 --> 01:08:38,282
(He even went to Germany to study percussion.)

1965
01:08:38,283 --> 01:08:41,922
I started when I was 17, so it's been 36 years.

1966
01:08:41,923 --> 01:08:44,482
(He played the timpani for 36 years, now as a member of the orchestra.)

1967
01:08:44,483 --> 01:08:45,623
That's amazing.

1968
01:08:46,053 --> 01:08:47,722
I'm sorry,

1969
01:08:47,723 --> 01:08:49,162
but how much is the timpani?

1970
01:08:49,163 --> 01:08:52,663
I know the Incheon Philharmonic's timpani is over 100,000 dollars.

1971
01:08:53,293 --> 01:08:54,762
And 1 set is consists of 5.

1972
01:08:54,763 --> 01:08:55,763
Gosh. That's...

1973
01:08:57,003 --> 01:08:58,403
Practicing it must be intimidating.

1974
01:08:58,973 --> 01:09:01,072
- Gosh. It's so expensive.
- Yes.

1975
01:09:01,073 --> 01:09:02,353
I'm treating it with great care.

1976
01:09:03,643 --> 01:09:06,172
Percussion instruments tend to be expensive.

1977
01:09:06,173 --> 01:09:07,893
The name of the instrument reminds me of...

1978
01:09:08,483 --> 01:09:10,182
- Today, it keeps popping up.
- It sounds familiar.

1979
01:09:10,183 --> 01:09:11,783
That's the vibe of today.

1980
01:09:12,453 --> 01:09:13,959
We came to Guwol-dong when it's September.

1981
01:09:13,983 --> 01:09:15,282
- Timpani and Tiffany.
- Timpani and Tiffany.

1982
01:09:15,283 --> 01:09:16,982
(Timpani, Tiffany)

1983
01:09:16,983 --> 01:09:18,923
We are rhyming like crazy today.

1984
01:09:19,623 --> 01:09:21,793
Everything fits perfectly, except for the weather.

1985
01:09:23,463 --> 01:09:24,862
It's so hot today.

1986
01:09:24,863 --> 01:09:27,632
I played the drums before.

1987
01:09:27,633 --> 01:09:28,862
- Yes.
- I saw it in the drama.

1988
01:09:28,863 --> 01:09:32,102
Yes. Compared to other instruments, it's more noticeable...

1989
01:09:32,103 --> 01:09:34,172
when you make a mistake when you play the percussion.

1990
01:09:34,173 --> 01:09:35,173
Of course.

1991
01:09:35,174 --> 01:09:37,272
Have you ever made mistakes while performing?

1992
01:09:37,273 --> 01:09:38,572
- Have you made mistakes?
- Yes.

1993
01:09:38,573 --> 01:09:42,012
It's going to be tough talking about it.

1994
01:09:42,013 --> 01:09:43,412
I'm still traumatized by it.

1995
01:09:43,413 --> 01:09:44,752
What happened?

1996
01:09:44,753 --> 01:09:46,882
Maestro Gum was conducting the orchestra.

1997
01:09:46,883 --> 01:09:48,523
- Maestro Gum Nanse.
- Maestro Gum Nanse.

1998
01:09:48,723 --> 01:09:50,683
Toward the end of the song, I played the timpani.

1999
01:09:51,353 --> 01:09:53,022
It was supposed to go slowly as the piece went by.

2000
01:09:53,023 --> 01:09:55,362
Da-da-da.

2001
01:09:55,363 --> 01:09:56,492
(After slowing down, he had to strike it to finish.)

2002
01:09:56,493 --> 01:09:59,093
I'd strike it hard after playing the timpani.

2003
01:09:59,733 --> 01:10:01,602
I was supposed to look at the maestro.

2004
01:10:01,603 --> 01:10:03,833
I just thought, "Why are we slowing down so much?"

2005
01:10:04,603 --> 01:10:06,372
"Yes, this is about the time." And I slammed it.

2006
01:10:06,373 --> 01:10:08,013
But the maestro's hand was still up here.

2007
01:10:08,773 --> 01:10:11,072
I slammed it, then it was followed by others.

2008
01:10:11,073 --> 01:10:12,542
- You had a head start.
- Yes.

2009
01:10:12,543 --> 01:10:13,872
- At that time...
- What happened?

2010
01:10:13,873 --> 01:10:15,282
- I came to see him.
- I see.

2011
01:10:15,283 --> 01:10:18,653
And he said, "I dragged a bit, didn't I?"

2012
01:10:19,453 --> 01:10:20,853
Are you doing an impression of him?

2013
01:10:21,383 --> 01:10:22,482
(Laughing)

2014
01:10:22,483 --> 01:10:25,093
I've never seen an impression of Maestro Gum.

2015
01:10:27,423 --> 01:10:29,423
He has a unique way of speaking.

2016
01:10:30,263 --> 01:10:31,993
If you like it, then it's fine.

2017
01:10:32,393 --> 01:10:33,532
(He loves it.)

2018
01:10:33,533 --> 01:10:36,232
Well, since you're playing the percussion,

2019
01:10:36,233 --> 01:10:38,002
do you have any occupational habits?

2020
01:10:38,003 --> 01:10:39,172
Gosh. My occupational habits.

2021
01:10:39,173 --> 01:10:41,232
When I walk on the streets,

2022
01:10:41,233 --> 01:10:42,772
I don't just walk, I split the beat.

2023
01:10:42,773 --> 01:10:44,102
- I see.
- You split the rhythm.

2024
01:10:44,103 --> 01:10:45,712
One, two, three, four.

2025
01:10:45,713 --> 01:10:46,842
One, two, three.

2026
01:10:46,843 --> 01:10:47,972
One, and two, and three.

2027
01:10:47,973 --> 01:10:49,613
One, ta-ta, two, ta-ta, three, ta-ta.

2028
01:10:49,783 --> 01:10:51,612
I split the beats like that.

2029
01:10:51,613 --> 01:10:53,552
Before a drama begins, there's a countdown...

2030
01:10:53,553 --> 01:10:54,852
on the screen.

2031
01:10:54,853 --> 01:10:57,023
I go like this, "One, and two, and..."

2032
01:10:57,423 --> 01:10:58,722
- Also with the turn signal.
- It's all about beats.

2033
01:10:58,723 --> 01:11:00,492
You always think about the beat.

2034
01:11:00,493 --> 01:11:01,722
Yes. That's what I do.

2035
01:11:01,723 --> 01:11:03,162
(He's always obsessed with the beat.)

2036
01:11:03,163 --> 01:11:06,232
By the way, what made you...

2037
01:11:06,233 --> 01:11:07,932
send the letter to us?

2038
01:11:07,933 --> 01:11:09,862
I love watching TV so much.

2039
01:11:09,863 --> 01:11:12,932
- Really?
- I love dramas and variety shows.

2040
01:11:12,933 --> 01:11:16,372
I'm always watching TV during my break...

2041
01:11:16,373 --> 01:11:18,572
- if I'm not studying.
- You're a TV aficionado.

2042
01:11:18,573 --> 01:11:19,573
- Yes.
- I see.

2043
01:11:19,574 --> 01:11:21,982
I was watching Whenever Possible,

2044
01:11:21,983 --> 01:11:24,642
and I thought, "Will I be able to do that?"

2045
01:11:24,643 --> 01:11:26,182
And at the end, the caption appeared...

2046
01:11:26,183 --> 01:11:27,352
for those who want to wend the letter.

2047
01:11:27,353 --> 01:11:28,553
- At the bottom.
- Yes.

2048
01:11:28,823 --> 01:11:31,083
So, I wrote the letter about two years ago.

2049
01:11:31,453 --> 01:11:32,453
(2 years ago?)

2050
01:11:32,454 --> 01:11:33,793
- Really?
- Two years ago?

2051
01:11:35,363 --> 01:11:38,022
It must be surprising to get the call after two years.

2052
01:11:38,023 --> 01:11:40,493
I hung up because it was from a number I didn't know.

2053
01:11:41,063 --> 01:11:42,462
- You thought it was a prank.
- Yes.

2054
01:11:42,463 --> 01:11:44,003
Then I got a text right away.

2055
01:11:44,703 --> 01:11:45,903
So, I called at once.

2056
01:11:46,133 --> 01:11:47,503
You are full of fun stories.

2057
01:11:48,003 --> 01:11:49,173
Am I?

2058
01:11:49,903 --> 01:11:52,672
Anyway, now that we're here...

2059
01:11:52,673 --> 01:11:54,313
in Guwol-dong,

2060
01:11:54,643 --> 01:11:56,512
- I want to wrap it up nicely.
- Yes, we should.

2061
01:11:56,513 --> 01:11:57,942
Sons of Incheon,

2062
01:11:57,943 --> 01:12:00,912
- Yo Han and Jae Won, are here.
- Two Sons of Incheon.

2063
01:12:00,913 --> 01:12:02,883
